# Build Specification — DEBUG//LAKSHAY

You are building a highly interactive personal portfolio website for Lakshay Batra, a software engineer.

This is NOT a conventional portfolio website.

The website should feel like a real piece of software created by an engineer who enjoys debugging, systems, code, experimentation, and technology.

The portfolio must be visually memorable, technically polished, fast, responsive, and completely free to deploy.

---

# 1. OVERALL PRODUCT CONCEPT

The portfolio has two distinct phases:

## Phase 1 — Opening Experience

When the visitor first opens the website, they do NOT immediately see a normal portfolio.

Instead:

```text
WEBSITE OPENS
      ↓
BOOT SEQUENCE
      ↓
SYSTEM INITIALIZATION
      ↓
SYSTEM DIAGNOSTICS
      ↓
⚠ ERROR DETECTED
      ↓
DSA DEBUGGING CHALLENGE
      ↓
SOLVE THE BUG
      ↓
✓ SYSTEM RESTORED
      ↓
MAIN PORTFOLIO
```

The opening experience is the unique hook of the website.

The visitor sees an old-school terminal/computer-style boot sequence.

The system appears to initialize the portfolio.

After initialization, it reports an error.

The visitor is shown a small DSA/code problem containing a deliberate bug.

They must identify the problematic line or make a very small correction.

Once the problem is solved, the system says that the issue has been resolved and transitions into the actual portfolio.

There should also be a humorous escape option such as:

```text
[ DEBUG ]

I'm dumb, let me in →
```

Clicking this skips the challenge and opens the portfolio.

IMPORTANT:

The debugging concept exists ONLY during the opening experience.

After entering the main portfolio, DO NOT force the visitor to debug things to navigate the site.

The main portfolio should be a normal, highly polished portfolio with a technical visual identity.

---

# 2. MAIN PORTFOLIO VISION

After the boot/debug sequence, the website becomes a normal portfolio.

The main sections will eventually include:

- Home
- Projects
- Experience
- Skills / Technologies
- GitHub / Engineering Activity
- Resume
- Contact

The main portfolio should still visually communicate:

- software engineering
- programming
- systems
- AI/ML
- backend development
- experimentation
- technical curiosity

But it should NOT look like a generic:

```text
Name
Software Engineer

About me...

Skills:
React
Node
Python
...
```

Avoid generic SaaS landing-page aesthetics.

Avoid excessive gradients.

Avoid generic glassmorphism.

Avoid the typical "developer portfolio" template look.

The website should feel like an engineer designed an interface, not like a designer placed code snippets on a landing page.

---

# 3. VISUAL IDENTITY

The visual language established during the boot sequence must be compatible with the entire website.

The boot should establish the following design language:

- dark interface
- monochrome / restrained colors
- terminal-inspired typography
- technical UI elements
- subtle CRT/computer influence
- system status indicators
- thin borders
- monospace typography where appropriate
- subtle green/amber/red diagnostic accents
- minimal but purposeful animation
- precise spacing
- technical information hierarchy

IMPORTANT:

Do NOT turn the entire portfolio into a retro terminal.

The boot can be strongly terminal-inspired.

The main portfolio should evolve this aesthetic into a modern engineering interface.

Think:

"modern software engineering workstation"

rather than:

"1980s hacker movie."

---

# 4. DESIGN PHILOSOPHY

The website should communicate technical ability through the interface itself.

Do NOT rely on huge text saying:

"I am a software engineer."

Instead, demonstrate it through:

- interactive components
- architecture visualizations
- real project information
- code
- technical decisions
- GitHub activity
- system diagrams
- project metrics
- thoughtful interactions

The website itself should feel like a software project.

---

# 5. TECH STACK

The entire project must remain free to build and deploy.

Use:

- Next.js
- React
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- Motion / Framer Motion
- Monaco Editor when the debugging editor is implemented
- React Flow later for interactive architecture diagrams
- Lucide React for icons
- GitHub API later if live GitHub data is added

Do NOT introduce paid services.

Do NOT introduce a database unless genuinely necessary.

Do NOT introduce an external backend at this stage.

The initial application should be able to run completely locally and deploy using a free hosting platform.

