---

# 8. COMPONENT 1 — BOOT EXPERIENCE

The boot should begin immediately when the website loads.

Initial screen:

```text
DEBUG//LAKSHAY

> Initializing system...
```

Use a dark, nearly black background.

The visual feeling should be:

- old computer terminal
- engineering workstation
- system diagnostics
- subtle CRT

Do not use a generic loading spinner.

---

# 9. BOOT SEQUENCE

Target duration:

Approximately 4–6 seconds.

Do NOT make it unnecessarily long.

The visitor should be able to understand what is happening.

Logs should appear progressively.

Example:

```text
DEBUG//LAKSHAY
────────────────────────

> Initializing portfolio kernel...
✓ Portfolio kernel initialized

> Loading project registry...
✓ Project registry loaded

> Loading engineering profile...
✓ Profile loaded

> Initializing diagnostic engine...
✓ Diagnostic engine ready

> Connecting to repository layer...
✓ Repository layer ready

> Running system diagnostics...
```

The exact messages can be adjusted during implementation.

---

# 10. REAL INITIALIZATION

The boot screen should not be a fake animation.

Where practical, actual application initialization should occur behind the scenes.

Examples:

- initialize portfolio data
- initialize project registry
- initialize diagnostic configuration
- prepare application state
- preload relevant assets
- prepare future debugger state

If GitHub integration is eventually added, its data may be initialized later.

Do not make the boot wait indefinitely for external APIs.

The boot should have a graceful fallback.

---

# 11. BOOT STATE MACHINE

Do not implement the boot as a collection of arbitrary `setTimeout()` calls.

Use a clear state machine / task-based architecture.

Conceptually:

```text
BOOT_START
    ↓
INITIALIZE_PROFILE
    ↓
LOAD_PROJECTS
    ↓
INITIALIZE_DIAGNOSTICS
    ↓
PREPARE_APPLICATION
    ↓
RUN_DIAGNOSTICS
    ↓
BOOT_COMPLETE
```

Each task should have a status such as:

```text
pending
running
success
warning
error
```

The visual layer should render these states.

This will make the system extensible when future components are added.

---

# 12. TERMINAL LOG DESIGN

Each operation should look like a real system log.

Use:

```text
> operation...
```

while running.

Then:

```text
✓ operation complete
```

on success.

Warnings:

```text
⚠ warning
```

Errors:

```text
✕ error
```

Use color sparingly.

The UI should primarily be monochrome, with colors reserved for system state.

---

# 13. PLAYFUL LOGS

Occasionally include humorous developer-oriented logs.

Examples:

```text
> Checking caffeine levels...
✓ Acceptable

> Searching for bugs...
⚠ This may take a while

> Checking sleep schedule...
⚠ Module unavailable

> Compiling personality...
✓ Mostly successful

> Initializing Lakshay.exe...
✓ Process started
```

These should be rare.

The tone should be clever and understated.

Do NOT make every log a joke.

The website should still feel professional.

