---

# 15. CRT EFFECT

Use a subtle CRT treatment.

Possible effects:

- faint scanlines
- very subtle screen noise
- slight text glow
- blinking terminal cursor
- tiny amount of flicker

Avoid:

- extreme VHS effects
- heavy distortion
- constant glitching
- unreadable text
- excessive green
- flashing screens

The visual effect should enhance the concept rather than become the concept.

---

# 16. TYPOGRAPHY

Use a high-quality monospace font for the boot environment.

The typography should feel like a developer terminal.

Possible font:

JetBrains Mono or another appropriate open-source monospace font.

Do not use a novelty "hacker" font.

Readable > gimmicky.

---

# 17. TERMINAL CURSOR

Include a blinking cursor.

Example:

```text
> Running diagnostics_█
```

The cursor should:

- blink subtly
- stop or transition naturally when boot completes
- respect reduced-motion preferences

---

# 18. FINAL DIAGNOSTIC

The final boot operation should reveal that something is wrong.

Example:

```text
> Running system diagnostics...

✓ Projects
✓ Experience
✓ Repository
⚠ Diagnostic integrity

3 anomalies detected.
```

Then show:

```text
────────────────────────────────

SYSTEM DIAGNOSTICS COMPLETE

⚠ ERROR DETECTED

Portfolio integrity: 87%

Investigation required.

────────────────────────────────
```

This is the bridge into COMPONENT 2:

## DSA DEBUGGING CHALLENGE

Do NOT implement the challenge yet.

Only create the transition/state interface required for it.

---

# 19. TRANSITION TO DEBUGGER

The boot should not simply disappear.

Create a visually coherent transition.

Suggested:

```text
SYSTEM DIAGNOSTICS COMPLETE

3 anomalies detected.

        ↓

screen briefly flickers

        ↓

terminal interface reorganizes

        ↓

DEBUGGER SCREEN
```

The visitor should feel that the system has discovered a problem and handed control to a diagnostic environment.

The transition should preserve the same visual language.

---

# 20. NO SKIP ON BOOT

There is NO skip button for the boot sequence.

The boot is part of the experience.

However:

- keep it short
- never wait indefinitely
- have fallback behavior
- do not block the user unnecessarily

The later DSA debugging challenge WILL have a skip option.

---

# 21. RESPONSIVE DESIGN

The boot must work on:

- desktop
- laptop
- tablet
- mobile

On mobile, simplify the layout.

Example:

```text
DEBUG//LAKSHAY

> Initializing...
✓ Profile loaded
✓ Projects loaded
✓ Diagnostics ready

⚠ 3 anomalies detected
```

Do not try to cram a fake desktop interface into a mobile screen.

---

# 22. ACCESSIBILITY

Implement:

- sufficient text contrast
- semantic status information
- reduced-motion support
- no dangerous flashing
- readable font sizes
- keyboard accessibility where applicable

For users with reduced motion enabled:

- remove CRT flicker
- remove unnecessary transitions
- use simple fades

The information should remain understandable without animation.

---

# 23. PERFORMANCE

The boot system must be lightweight.

Do NOT add:

- Three.js
- video backgrounds
- large image assets
- unnecessary libraries
- heavy visual effects

The boot should load quickly.

Avoid blocking the page on external API calls.

---

# 24. FAILURE HANDLING

If a real initialization task fails:

Example:

```text
> Loading repository metadata...
⚠ Repository unavailable

✓ Continuing in offline mode
```

The application must continue.

Never allow:

```text
Loading...
```

to remain indefinitely.

Set reasonable timeouts/fallbacks.

---

# 25. CODE QUALITY

Follow good engineering practices.

Requirements:

- TypeScript
- strongly typed states
- reusable components
- no unnecessary `any`
- no duplicated logic
- no giant component
- clear naming
- clean separation between boot logic and visual rendering
- no hardcoded state scattered throughout JSX
- comments only where they add value

The boot system should be something that another developer could understand and extend.

---

# 26. Future Compatibility

The boot system must eventually connect to:

```text
Boot
 ↓
Diagnostics
 ↓
DSA Debugger
 ↓
Main Portfolio
```

Create a clean interface/state boundary for this.

For example, conceptually:

```typescript
type BootCompletion = {
  status: "complete";
  diagnostics: {
    anomalyCount: number;
  };
};
```

The exact implementation is up to you.

Do NOT hardcode the future debugger into the boot component.

